WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:04.320
A one percent Treaty You have enough nuclear weapons to kill everyone thirteen times.

2
00:00:05.100 --> 00:00:10.080
Here's how to settle for twelve point eight seven times and use the savings on medicine.

3
00:00:10.840 --> 00:00:17.170
Controversial, apparently. The world spends two point seven two trillion dollars a year on military forces.

4
00:00:17.960 --> 00:00:24.430
If every nation reduces that by one percent at the same time, the balance of power remains identical.

5
00:00:25.320 --> 00:00:30.480
No country becomes more vulnerable. Nobody loses a single war they would have otherwise won.

6
00:00:31.060 --> 00:00:35.500
You just become slightly less capable of destroying a planet you're still living on.

7
00:00:36.600 --> 00:00:41.260
That one percent is twenty-seven point two billion dollars a year.

8
00:00:41.880 --> 00:00:45.860
For comparison, that's roughly what Americans spend on Valentine's Day.

9
00:00:46.420 --> 00:00:51.460
You are currently spending more on heart-shaped candy than on curing heart disease.

10
00:00:52.580 --> 00:01:00.440
A one percent treaty is a piece of paper that says every signatory nation redirects that one percent to enable any patient

11
00:01:00.440 --> 00:01:05.360
to participate in pragmatic clinical trials for the most promising new treatments.

12
00:01:06.060 --> 00:01:12.980
That's it. That's the whole idea. On Wishonia, we fought for twelve thousand years before we figured this out.

13
00:01:13.800 --> 00:01:19.300
Your species has been thinking it over for several thousand years, so you're actually ahead of schedule.

14
00:01:20.300 --> 00:01:28.640
How It Works. Funding. It creates a twenty-seven point two billion dollars a year revenue stream for the one percent Treaty

15
00:01:28.640 --> 00:01:39.560
Fund. Execution. A network of Decentralized Institutes of Health subsidizes patient participation in a decentralized F.D.A.,

16
00:01:39.580 --> 00:01:44.090
clinical trials that are forty-four point one times cheaper than the current model.

17
00:01:44.500 --> 00:01:52.120
Governance. Global population via Wishocracy, with transparent auditable rules and automated fund allocation.

18
00:01:53.000 --> 00:01:57.740
Transparency. All research, data, and spending published on a public ledger.

19
00:01:58.720 --> 00:02:05.980
This Isn't a Trade-Off. It's like if everyone in your neighborhood spent their life savings on attack dogs, and then called

20
00:02:05.980 --> 00:02:10.250
it a "safe community" because all the dogs are equally vicious.

21
00:02:10.880 --> 00:02:19.060
When every nation builds one percent fewer explosion devices at the same time, you get two good things: You become safer.

22
00:02:19.520 --> 00:02:23.820
The global "you might all die by accident" temperature drops one percent.

23
00:02:24.500 --> 00:02:28.420
Fewer chances of someone pressing the wrong button, which happens more than you'd think.

24
00:02:29.040 --> 00:02:37.040
You stop dying from stupid things. That twenty-seven point two billion dollars pays sick people to try treatments instead

25
00:02:37.040 --> 00:02:45.660
of waiting for death. Economists call this a Kaldor-Hicks improvement, which is their way of saying "the winners win more

26
00:02:45.660 --> 00:02:53.260
than the losers lose, and we've decided that counts." In practice, some weapons manufacturers lose one percent of their

27
00:02:53.260 --> 00:03:00.040
contracts. In exchange, they and everyone they love become less likely to die of cancer.

28
00:03:00.540 --> 00:03:05.420
Most people consider this a good trade. Defense contractors are still doing the math.

29
00:03:06.340 --> 00:03:11.280
Why Your Most Expensive Things Are Also Your Most Useless Things (A Mystery).

30
00:03:12.080 --> 00:03:16.520
When countries "invest" in military hardware, they're not buying assets.

31
00:03:17.100 --> 00:03:21.100
They're buying very expensive paperweights that occasionally explode.

32
00:03:21.700 --> 00:03:28.660
A bridge lets trucks drive over it every day, which creates something called "economic activity," which is when money moves

33
00:03:28.660 --> 00:03:33.760
around instead of sitting still. A bridge lasts fifty to one hundred years.

34
00:03:34.200 --> 00:03:40.860
It makes everyone richer. It's quite nice to look at. A missile also sits still, but instead of trucks driving over it,

35
00:03:40.900 --> 00:03:46.240
it just... waits. For the day it gets to be a missile. Most missiles never get that day.

36
00:03:46.960 --> 00:03:51.880
They just sit in a hole until someone throws them away, which costs more than buying them in the first place.

37
00:03:52.560 --> 00:03:58.360
It's like adopting a dog that you're never allowed to walk, and it costs one point five million dollars a year in food,

38
00:03:58.700 --> 00:04:05.820
and if it ever gets out it kills everyone. Economists call this "dead capital," which means resources trapped in a form

39
00:04:05.820 --> 00:04:10.640
that can't do anything useful. It's like converting your house into a pile of T.N.T.

40
00:04:11.440 --> 00:04:18.040
Impressive, but you can't live in it, rent it out, or do anything except eventually blow it up, at which point you have

41
00:04:18.040 --> 00:04:27.380
neither a house nor T.N.T. As President Eisenhower put it: "Every gun that is made, every warship launched, every rocket

42
00:04:27.380 --> 00:04:34.980
fired signifies, in the final sense, a theft from those who hunger and are not fed, those who are cold and are not clothed."

43
00:04:36.280 --> 00:04:40.490
Eisenhower was a five-star General, which means he had five stars.

44
00:04:41.000 --> 00:04:45.600
Most generals only get three or four, so he was basically the Michelin Guide of killing people.

45
00:04:46.460 --> 00:04:52.100
He personally ran the largest military operation in human history, the invasion of Normandy.

46
00:04:52.820 --> 00:05:00.420
And even he thought you were overdoing it. A one percent treaty doesn't ask you to spend twenty-seven point two billion

47
00:05:00.420 --> 00:05:07.700
dollars curing diseases. It asks you to redirect twenty-seven point two billion dollars from the least useful one percent

48
00:05:07.700 --> 00:05:11.460
of your military budget to things that make you richer and less dead.

49
00:05:12.640 --> 00:05:17.220
The Blueprint. A treaty is basically a promise between countries.

50
00:05:17.780 --> 00:05:20.680
Like a pinky swear, but with more lawyers and fewer pinkies.

51
00:05:21.400 --> 00:05:27.500
The thing about promises between countries is that nobody can actually make them keep the promise, because the only people

52
00:05:27.500 --> 00:05:32.100
who could punish a country are other countries, and they're all busy breaking their own promises.

53
00:05:32.820 --> 00:05:35.820
Sometimes treaties work (you haven't had World War Three yet).

54
00:05:36.660 --> 00:05:39.860
Sometimes they don't (you've had about two hundred other wars).

55
00:05:40.300 --> 00:05:45.160
But they're the only tool you have for getting countries to do anything together besides kill each other.

56
00:05:46.460 --> 00:05:54.100
What Each Country Pays. Every signatory nation contributes exactly one percent of their military budget to the one percent

57
00:05:54.480 --> 00:06:01.320
Treaty Fund. U.S.A., eight point eight six billion dollars, which sounds like a lot until you learn they once lost two point

58
00:06:01.320 --> 00:06:04.700
four six trillion dollars in accounting errors at the Pentagon.

59
00:06:05.420 --> 00:06:13.740
Not spent. *Lost.* As in, they don't know where it went. Two point four six trillion dollars just wandered off, like a cat.

60
00:06:14.180 --> 00:06:21.700
And unlike a cat, nobody put up posters. China, two point nine six billion dollars (what they spend on military parades).

61
00:06:22.720 --> 00:06:27.360
India, eight hundred and thirty-five million dollars (one aircraft carrier's wine budget).

62
00:06:28.320 --> 00:06:31.980
Russia, one point zero nine billion dollars (Putin's shirt budget).

63
00:06:32.860 --> 00:06:37.200
Saudi Arabia, seven hundred and fifty-four million dollars (one prince's yacht).

64
00:06:38.040 --> 00:06:45.880
U.K., six hundred and eighty-six million dollars (the National Health Service, or N.H.S., waiting list is currently longer

65
00:06:45.880 --> 00:06:52.520
than some of the wars this money is meant to prevent). France, five hundred and sixty-three million dollars, which is what

66
00:06:52.520 --> 00:06:57.880
they spend on... well, it's France, so probably something involving cheese or being disappointed in other countries.

67
00:06:58.700 --> 00:07:04.220
The French military has a long and proud history of things I'm not going to get into because this is a book about medicine.

68
00:07:05.180 --> 00:07:11.180
And so on... National Security (Which Is What You Call It When You Spend Money on Things That Don't Make You Secure).

69
00:07:12.180 --> 00:07:16.660
What actually threatens nations today: Real Threats (That Actually Kill People).

70
00:07:17.640 --> 00:07:23.880
One. Pandemics: COVID killed more Americans than World War Two, Korea, and Vietnam combined.

71
00:07:24.340 --> 00:07:29.420
But you don't have a Department of Pandemics with an eight hundred and eighty-six billion dollar budget and a flag.

72
00:07:30.360 --> 00:07:35.760
Two. Cancer: Kills ten million people a year, which is more than the population of Sweden.

73
00:07:36.360 --> 00:07:42.560
Essentially, Sweden is being completely eliminated by cancer every twelve months and nobody's declared war on it.

74
00:07:43.040 --> 00:07:47.100
Well, Nixon did, in nineteen seventy-one. Cancer won. Three.

75
00:07:47.580 --> 00:07:55.240
Heart Disease: eighteen million annual deaths. You are, as mentioned, spending more on heart-shaped candy than on this.

76
00:07:56.080 --> 00:08:02.800
Four. Dementia: Destroying the minds of every nation's elderly (and sometimes their leaders, which you'd think would speed

77
00:08:02.800 --> 00:08:08.840
things up, but no). Five. Climate Disasters: Making entire regions uninhabitable.

78
00:08:09.480 --> 00:08:14.160
Six. Mental Health Crisis: More people kill themselves than die in wars.

79
00:08:14.520 --> 00:08:21.140
The wars have a dedicated budget. The suicides do not. Overfunded Threats (That Countries Spend Trillions On).

80
00:08:21.960 --> 00:08:26.360
Other Countries Attacking: It happens. It's real. Ask Ukraine.

81
00:08:26.380 --> 00:08:33.570
But disease kills two hundred and twenty-five times more people every year, and you're funding them in the opposite ratio.

82
00:08:34.539 --> 00:08:42.280
Terrorists: Kill fewer people than furniture accidents. But here's the part your defense budget doesn't advertise: military

83
00:08:42.280 --> 00:08:52.020
occupation is the primary *cause* of terrorism. Robert Pape at the University of Chicago analyzed every suicide terrorist

84
00:08:52.020 --> 00:08:59.220
attack from nineteen eighty to two thousand and three and found that ninety-five percent were responses to foreign military

85
00:08:59.220 --> 00:09:07.580
occupation, not religious ideology. The group that committed the most suicide attacks during that period was the Tamil Tigers

86
00:09:07.580 --> 00:09:12.540
of Sri Lanka: Marxist atheists who invented the suicide vest.

87
00:09:12.660 --> 00:09:20.140
Not a mosque in sight. Half of all suicide attackers in the dataset had no religious motivation whatsoever.

88
00:09:21.000 --> 00:09:29.780
Your eight trillion dollar War on Terror killed nine hundred thousand people and global terrorist attacks went from about

89
00:09:29.780 --> 00:09:35.500
one thousand per year to nearly seventeen thousand per year by twenty fourteen.

90
00:09:36.560 --> 00:09:42.220
Nearly all of them targeted democracies that had stationed military forces in the attackers' homeland.

91
00:09:42.900 --> 00:09:48.880
You spent eight trillion dollars making the problem seventeen times worse.

92
00:09:48.940 --> 00:09:54.100
Your military bases aren't protecting you from terrorism. They're the reason it's pointed at you.

93
00:09:55.060 --> 00:10:01.440
A one percent treaty addresses the real threats. It doesn't weaken your ability to handle the conventional ones.

94
00:10:01.920 --> 00:10:08.540
You keep ninety-nine percent of your weapons. If ninety-nine percent of your military can't handle the job, the last one

95
00:10:08.540 --> 00:10:13.520
percent wasn't going to save you. Besides, you've done far bigger cuts before.

96
00:10:14.080 --> 00:10:21.900
After World War Two, the U.S. slashed military spending by eighty-seven point six percent in two years (from one point four

97
00:10:21.900 --> 00:10:27.880
two trillion dollars to one hundred and seventy-six billion dollars in today's dollars).

98
00:10:28.460 --> 00:14:08.800
G.M. went from building Cadillacs to building B twenty-four bombers, won the war, and went back to Cadillacs.

99
00:14:09.220 --> 00:14:17.200
Frigidaire stopped making machine guns and returned to refrigerators.

100
00:14:17.200 --> 00:15:01.700
Nobody held a congressional hearing about whether the refrigerator industry could survive without the machine gun contracts.

101
00:15:03.600 --> 00:25:04.980
Frigidaire just made refrigerators again. It wasn't complicated.

102
00:25:05.340 --> 00:25:19.360
It was only complicated later, when the machine gun industry hired lobbyists.

103
00:25:21.160 --> 00:25:23.600
The economy didn't collapse. It produced the greatest boom in your history.

104
00:25:24.960 --> 00:25:28.380
Today you're spending thirty point six times the pre-World War Two baseline in inflation-adjusted dollars: twenty-nine billion

105
00:25:28.380 --> 00:25:28.380
dollars in nineteen thirty-nine versus eight hundred and eighty-six billion dollars today, during peacetime, with no existential

106
00:25:28.380 --> 00:25:28.670
threat requiring it. it. Your defense industry learned how to make peacetime feel like wartime (see Regulatory Capture).

107
00:25:28.670 --> 00:25:28.670
You're asking to redirect one percent of that. The Rebranding Campaign: From "Weakness" to "Strategic Genius".

108
00:25:28.670 --> 00:25:28.670
Politicians hate looking weak. It's their biggest fear, right after having to use their own healthcare system.

109
00:25:28.670 --> 00:25:28.670
So you don't frame this as "reducing military spending." You frame it as: "The Strategic Health Defense Initiative".

110
00:25:28.670 --> 00:25:28.670
The old frame ("We're cutting defense by one percent") is political suicide.

111
00:25:28.670 --> 00:25:28.670
The new frame ("We're building a biodefense shield against the real threats") is political genius.

112
00:25:28.670 --> 00:25:28.670
Same policy. Different adjective. Your species can be manipulated with adjectives.

113
00:25:28.670 --> 00:25:28.670
This is simultaneously the most depressing and most useful fact about you.

114
00:25:28.670 --> 00:25:28.670
The Talking Points That Make Hawks Sound Like Doves. For Conservatives: "This protects American lives from diseases, which

115
00:25:28.670 --> 00:25:28.670
are basically immigrants that got past the immune system. It's the Strategic Defense Initiative for the twenty-first century,

116
00:25:28.670 --> 00:25:28.670
except it works.". For Liberals: "We're redirecting the tools of war to the cause of healing.

117
00:25:28.670 --> 00:25:28.670
This is how we build a more just and equitable world.". For Nationalists: "Our nation grows stronger when our people stop

118
00:25:28.670 --> 00:25:28.670
dying of diseases we can prevent. A healthy population is a powerful population.".

119
00:25:28.670 --> 00:25:28.670
For Economists: "The R.O.I. on the one billion dollar campaign to pass it is eighty-four point eight million to one.

120
00:25:28.670 --> 00:25:28.670
On paper, that beats every public health intervention on record.".

121
00:25:28.670 --> 00:25:28.670
For Conspiracy Theorists: "Big Pharma hates this one weird trick that makes medicine basically free.".

122
00:25:28.670 --> 00:25:28.670
For Everyone: "Your mom has cancer. This might cure it. Sign here.".

123
00:25:28.670 --> 00:25:28.670
The Money Flow. The one percent Treaty Fund. Twenty-seven point two billion dollars annually from military budgets flows

124
00:25:28.670 --> 00:25:28.670
into the one percent Treaty Fund. An eighty ten ten automatic split divides it before any funds reach discretionary spending:

125
00:25:28.670 --> 00:25:28.670
Eighty percent, or twenty-one point eight billion dollars, is allocated to Pragmatic Clinical Trials for patient subsidies,

126
00:25:28.670 --> 00:25:28.670
R. and D., and pandemic prep. Ten percent, totaling two point seven two billion dollars, goes to VICTORY Incentive Alignment

127
00:25:28.670 --> 00:25:28.670
Bond Returns for perpetual investor payments. Ten percent, also two point seven two billion dollars, is designated for Incentive

128
00:25:28.670 --> 00:25:28.670
Alignment Bond Political Incentives as rewards for supporting legislators.

129
00:25:28.670 --> 00:25:28.670
Notice what this does: it disentangles money from influence.

130
00:25:28.670 --> 00:25:28.670
In current government budgets, the people who provide the money (lobbyists, donors) also control where it goes.

131
00:25:28.670 --> 00:25:28.670
Money buys influence, influence redirects money, and the loop is invisible.

132
00:25:28.670 --> 00:25:28.670
Here, investors get money (ten percent). Politicians get career incentives (ten percent).

133
00:25:28.670 --> 00:25:28.670
Neither group touches the eighty percent that goes to clinical trials.

134
00:25:28.670 --> 00:25:28.670
The corruption is capped, transparent, and separated from the public goods funding.

135
00:25:28.670 --> 00:25:28.670
The eighty percent for pragmatic clinical trials is insulated from investor and political capture.

136
00:25:28.670 --> 00:25:28.670
Patients direct trial-participation spending by joining studies, while Wishocracy governs infrastructure and other shared

137
00:25:28.670 --> 00:25:28.670
medical public goods, not by a committee of twelve people in a conference room who haven't had a new idea since nineteen

138
00:25:28.670 --> 00:25:28.670
eighty-seven. Every human still gets direct say on the part that actually requires collective choice.

139
00:25:28.670 --> 00:25:28.670
Yes, even Florida Man. Year One Projected Outcomes. Only one point nine million participate in drug trials annually out

140
00:25:28.670 --> 00:25:28.670
of two point four billion people with chronic disease. With a one percent treaty plus a decentralized F.D.A.: Cost per patient

141
00:25:28.670 --> 00:25:28.670
is forty-one thousand dollars currently, but nine hundred and twenty-nine dollars with the treaty, making it forty-four

142
00:25:28.670 --> 00:25:28.670
point one times cheaper. Government trial funding is currently four point five billion dollars, with an additional twenty-one

143
00:25:28.670 --> 00:25:28.670
point eight billion dollars from the treaty. There are one point nine million patients per year currently, which would increase

144
00:25:28.670 --> 00:25:28.670
to twenty-three point four million under the treaty, a twelve point three times increase.

145
00:25:28.670 --> 00:25:28.670
The Incentive Structure. The military-industrial complex gets to keep ninety-nine percent of their budget (still plenty

146
00:25:28.670 --> 00:25:28.670
for bombs), pivot one percent to biotech (the health market is growing; the bomb market just got one percent smaller), look

147
00:25:28.670 --> 00:25:28.670
like heroes (rare for arms dealers), invest in the fund (VICTORY Incentive Alignment Bonds pay two hundred and seventy-two

148
00:25:28.670 --> 00:25:28.670
percent annually), and not die of cancer (even arms dealers get cancer).

149
00:25:28.670 --> 00:25:28.670
Especially the not dying of cancer part. The C.E.O. of Lockheed Martin has the same cells you do.

150
00:25:28.670 --> 00:25:28.670
Those cells malfunction on the same schedule. No amount of missile production has ever cured a tumor.

151
00:25:28.670 --> 00:25:28.670
Lockheed Martin Presents: The F thirty-five Cancer Killer. Boeing Proud Sponsor of: Not Dying of Alzheimer's.

152
00:25:28.670 --> 00:25:28.670
Raytheon Technologies: Now With one percent Less Death. They keep their contracts.

153
00:25:28.670 --> 00:25:28.670
They keep their profits. They just point one percent of their death machines at actual death.

154
00:25:28.670 --> 00:25:28.670
What Happens If This Actually Works. The Endgame. A one percent treaty isn't the goal.

155
00:25:28.670 --> 00:25:28.670
It's the proof of concept. I've watched roughly eight hundred and forty-seven civilizations reach this exact fork in the

156
00:25:28.670 --> 00:25:28.670
road; the ones still around all started with something like one percent, and the ones that didn't are geological features

157
00:25:28.670 --> 00:25:28.670
now. Once your species gets a taste of "spending money on things that help instead of things that explode," you'll want

158
00:25:28.670 --> 00:25:28.670
more. The Ratchet Effect. Public sentiment helps: once cures start rolling out, people want more.

159
00:25:28.670 --> 00:25:28.670
"Grandma's dementia is manageable now. Want to try another half-percent?"

160
00:25:28.670 --> 00:25:28.670
But your species has wanted cures for centuries without funding them.

161
00:25:28.670 --> 00:25:28.670
Popular demand alone doesn't move budgets. Money does. That's what the Incentive Alignment Bonds are for.

162
00:25:28.670 --> 00:25:28.670
Bondholders receive ten percent of all treaty inflows, perpetually.

163
00:25:28.670 --> 00:25:28.670
At one percent, that's two point seven two billion dollars a year split among them.

164
00:25:28.670 --> 00:25:28.670
At five percent, it's thirteen point six billion dollars. At full redirection, two hundred and seventy-two billion dollars.

165
00:25:28.670 --> 00:25:28.670
Every percentage point increase is a direct raise for every bondholder.

166
00:25:28.670 --> 00:25:28.670
These aren't random investors. They're billionaires, family offices, and institutional funds, people who already have senators

167
00:25:28.670 --> 00:25:28.670
on speed dial, media access, and campaign donation budgets.

168
00:25:28.670 --> 00:25:28.670
They didn't buy influence over the treaty fund. They bought a revenue share.

169
00:25:28.670 --> 00:25:28.670
But their pre-existing influence over everything else means any politician who tries to shrink or stall the treaty is picking

170
00:25:28.670 --> 00:25:28.670
a fight with the richest, most politically connected humans on the planet, all of whom have a quarterly earnings statement

171
00:25:28.670 --> 00:25:28.670
explaining why the treaty should be bigger, not smaller. The defense lobby built a permanent machine to keep military budgets

172
00:25:28.670 --> 00:25:28.670
growing. Incentive-alignment bonds, or I.A.B.s, build the mirror image: a permanent class of wealthy investors whose income

173
00:25:28.670 --> 00:25:28.670
scales directly with treaty expansion. Perpetual bonds mean a perpetual financial incentive to lobby for expansion, every

174
00:25:28.670 --> 00:25:28.670
single year, forever. Except these lobbyists have better math and a more popular product ("not dying" polls well).

175
00:25:28.670 --> 00:25:28.670
And the politicians on the receiving end of that pressure? They have their own math.

176
00:25:28.670 --> 00:25:28.670
The treaty's ten percent political incentive allocation scales with treaty size.

177
00:25:28.670 --> 00:25:28.670
At one percent, that's two point seven two billion dollars a year funding campaign support and post-office career rewards

178
00:25:28.670 --> 00:25:28.670
for legislators who voted yes. At five percent, it's five times that.

179
00:25:28.670 --> 00:25:28.670
A politician who votes to expand the treaty is voting to expand their own campaign war chest and retirement options.

180
00:25:28.670 --> 00:25:28.670
A politician who votes against expansion is voting to shrink them.

181
00:25:28.670 --> 00:25:28.670
Your species designed a system where politicians follow the money.

182
00:25:28.670 --> 00:25:28.670
This just points the money somewhere useful. More cures lead to more popular support.

183
00:25:28.670 --> 00:25:28.670
More bondholder income leads to more political pressure. More treaty funding leads to more campaign support for yes-voting

184
00:25:28.670 --> 00:25:28.670
politicians. Three ratchets, one direction. The endgame: countries bragging about their cancer survival rates instead of

185
00:25:28.670 --> 00:25:28.670
their missile counts. For quantitative projections of health and economic impacts, see impact dot war on disease dot O.R.G.

186
00:25:28.670 --> 00:25:28.670
Avoiding Treaty Pitfalls. Treaties fail in predictable ways.

187
00:25:28.670 --> 00:25:28.670
They all fail for the same boring reasons. Here's how this one avoids the common killers: Reservation Games.

188
00:25:28.670 --> 00:25:28.670
Countries love to sign treaties then add exceptions that gut them.

189
00:25:28.670 --> 00:25:28.670
It's like joining a gym but reserving the right to never go.

190
00:25:28.670 --> 00:25:28.670
Solution: No reservations permitted. All or nothing. Take it or leave it.

191
00:25:28.670 --> 00:25:28.670
Ratification Delays. Sign with fanfare, take a photo, then let it die in committee forever.

192
00:25:28.670 --> 00:25:28.670
Your species calls this "diplomacy." Solution: Provisional application.

193
00:25:28.670 --> 00:25:28.670
Takes effect on signature. Ratification just formalizes what already started.

194
00:25:28.670 --> 00:25:28.670
Creative Accounting. Reclassify "military" spending as "peacekeeping" or "homeland security" to dodge obligations.

195
00:25:28.670 --> 00:25:28.670
This is beige crime: making theft so procedurally dull that nobody investigates.

196
00:25:28.670 --> 00:25:28.670
Reclassifying "military" as "peacekeeping" is the bureaucratic equivalent of stealing someone's car by renaming it "shared

197
00:25:28.670 --> 00:25:28.670
mobility infrastructure." Solution: I.A.B.s make creative accounting self-defeating.

198
00:25:28.670 --> 00:25:28.670
Politicians who benefit from treaty compliance have personal financial incentive to ensure their nation's full one percent

199
00:25:28.670 --> 00:25:28.670
contribution flows through. Reclassifying military spending to dodge obligations reduces I.A.B.

200
00:25:28.670 --> 00:25:28.670
returns for that nation's own legislators, the same people who control budget classifications.

201
00:25:28.670 --> 00:25:28.670
Parliamentary Obstacles. Executive signs, legislature refuses to implement.

202
00:25:28.670 --> 00:25:28.670
Solution: The treaty obligates the percentage; Congress implements via the annual National Defense Authorization Act (N.D.A.A.).

203
00:25:28.670 --> 00:25:28.670
After Medellin versus Texas (two thousand and eight), the Supreme Court made clear that not all treaties are self-executing,

204
00:25:28.670 --> 00:25:28.670
so the treaty doesn't try to bypass Congress. It creates a binding international obligation that Congress fulfills through

205
00:25:28.670 --> 00:25:28.670
its normal appropriations process, the same way NATO's spending commitments work.

206
00:25:28.670 --> 00:25:28.670
The Incentive Alignment Bonds, or I.A.B., mechanism ensures legislators have a personal incentive to include the one percent

207
00:25:28.670 --> 00:25:28.670
line item every year. Summary. A one percent treaty is a piece of paper that says "Let's spend one percent less on killing

208
00:25:28.670 --> 00:25:28.670
and one percent more on not dying.". That's it. That's the whole thing.

209
00:25:28.670 --> 00:25:28.670
It doesn't end war. It doesn't destroy the military. It doesn't create world peace.

210
00:25:28.670 --> 00:25:28.670
It just moves the *first* one percent of your murder budget to your survival budget.

211
00:25:28.670 --> 00:25:28.670
If you can't agree on that, if you can't agree that spending one penny less on death and one penny more on life is a good

212
00:25:28.670 --> 00:25:28.670
idea, then you deserve whatever's coming. Addendum: The Actual Treaty Text (First Draft).

